<section class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why · Tested</p>
    <h1>It is new. <span class="quiet">Here is what runs before it ships.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A young framework is fair to doubt. So instead of asking you to trust it,
      this page shows the checks: what they run, what fails them, and the
      commands to run them yourself. Every sample below is a transcript, not a
      paraphrase.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── 1 · One CI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="block">
  <div class="container split-proof">
    <div>
      <p class="num">1</p>
      <h2>CI is one script, and it runs on your laptop.</h2>
      <p>
        <code>bun run ci</code> is the whole of CI. GitHub Actions calls that one
        file and does nothing else, so a green run on a laptop and a green run on
        GitHub are the same run. It has sixteen phases, from lint and typecheck to
        a real deploy.
      </p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li><strong>No quiet skips.</strong> Every exception is a named entry with a reason in <code>scripts/ci-allowances.json</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Known failures are named</strong>, so a new one is loud instead of lost in the noise.</li>
        <li><strong>Every run writes its summary to a file</strong>, so a failure can be read back instead of reproduced.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <pre class="code">{@html sample.PHASES}</pre>
      <figcaption>The phase list, printed by the script itself.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── 2 · What you install ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="block">
  <div class="container split-proof">
    <div>
      <p class="num">2</p>
      <h2>We test what you install, not just our repo.</h2>
      <p>
        A monorepo can pass every test and still ship a broken package. So CI packs
        every publishable package into a tarball, creates a new app from those
        tarballs, installs it and builds it. Then it deploys that app to a real
        machine, crashes it, resumes it and rolls it back.
      </p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li><strong>The tutorial runs in CI.</strong> All thirteen <code>fli tutor</code> lessons execute end to end on every full run.</li>
        <li><strong>The docs run too.</strong> Every code block in Litestone's hand-written pages is built or executed, and a broken example is filed as a bug.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <pre class="code">{@html sample.INSTALL}</pre>
      <figcaption>The scaffold and deploy checks, from a full CI run's log.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── 3 · Depth ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="block">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="num">3</p>
    <h2>26,000 tests, and 97 drives against real browsers and servers.</h2>
    <p class="wide">
      Every package has its own suite, and every suite runs on every full CI run.
      The house rule is that behavior crossing packages is tested against the
      real dependency, not a fake client. Compiler tests parse their own output,
      because a clean compile is not proof of valid JavaScript.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Realm</th><th class="n">Tests passed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>litestone</td><td>Data</td><td class="n">8,092</td></tr>
          <tr><td>cli</td><td>Tooling</td><td class="n">4,326</td></tr>
          <tr><td>junction</td><td>API</td><td class="n">3,483</td></tr>
          <tr><td>mesa</td><td>UI</td><td class="n">2,588</td></tr>
          <tr><td>sierra</td><td>UI</td><td class="n">2,480</td></tr>
          <tr><td>basecamp</td><td>Deployment</td><td class="n">1,147</td></tr>
          <tr><td>auth</td><td>API</td><td class="n">685</td></tr>
          <tr><td>everything else</td><td>—</td><td class="n">3,342</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><td>Total</td><td></td><td class="n">26,143</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="split-proof drives">
      <div>
        <p>
          Unit tests are not enough for a framework that spans database to browser.
          A <strong>drive</strong> starts the real app and proves a flow end to end,
          in a real browser when the flow is a page: clicking, typing, and reading
          the console for errors.
          <code>fli proves</code> reads your diff and names the drives your change
          needs, and <code>fli done</code> will not call it finished until they pass.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.PROVES}</pre>
        <figcaption>One change's answer: the file it touched, and what it owes.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── 4 · Mutation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="block">
  <div class="container split-proof">
    <div>
      <p class="num">4</p>
      <h2>We test the tests.</h2>
      <p>
        A large test count can still miss the one rule that matters. In FrontierJS,
        who may read and change what is declared in the schema and enforced at the
        data layer. So <code>litestone mutate</code> breaks the schema one rule at a
        time — drops a deny, stores an <code>@encrypted</code> field in plaintext,
        loosens a relation's access — and checks that something catches each break.
      </p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li><strong>A break nothing catches is a rule nothing tests</strong>, and it is reported by name.</li>
        <li><strong>The floor is 100%.</strong> CI fails if the share of breaks caught drops below it, and that floor can only go up.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <pre class="code">{@html sample.MUTATE}</pre>
      <figcaption>Mutation testing on the example app's schema.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── 5 · Ratchets and gaps ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="block">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="num">5</p>
    <h2>Quality only moves one way, and the gaps are written down.</h2>
    <div class="split-proof">
      <div>
        <p>
          <strong>Ratchets.</strong> Every package typechecks with zero errors
          except one, whose ceiling is written in a file. That number can only go
          down; raising it shows up in a diff and needs a reason.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Nineteen rules, each with an enforcer.</strong> The architecture
          rests on nineteen invariants, and a generated file names what enforces
          each one. Where coverage is partial it says so in plain words, instead of
          looking the same as a rule that is fully enforced.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Defects and decisions are public.</strong> Every known defect has
          an id in <code>ISSUES.md</code> and every ruling one in
          <code>DECISIONS.md</code>, both in the repo.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-proof">
        <figure>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.RATCHET}</pre>
          <figcaption><code>scripts/typecheck-baselines.json</code>: one package with a ceiling, every other at zero.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.GAP}</pre>
          <figcaption>A gap, written down: from <code>invariants.snapshot.md</code>.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── The run this was written from ───────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="block">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>The run this page was written from was not green.</h2>
    <p class="wide">
      The numbers above come from a full CI run on the release commit
      <code>3e4f6c4b2</code>. Every phase passed except the test suites, where
      26,143 tests passed and <strong>3 failed</strong>: two of Litestone's CLI
      smoke tests and one Sierra test of search timing. CI named all three by
      test name, and this page will say so until a green run replaces it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── Run it yourself ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="block">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
    <p class="wide">Clone the repo and every claim on this page is one command away.</p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.RUN}</pre>
    <p class="see">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/docs/CI.md">what fails each phase</a> ·
